    "turn_text": "to go. Yeah, I guess you got to have three friends with him. He's been good to me. Like I say, I call him all the time when I see a turkey or a deer or something asking what I'm supposed to be doing. And he usually tells me just to get out of the way and let them do their business. So that's that's pretty cool. But we're on the we're on the Tennessee River Fort Loud. And if you live here, we're on the in the shade of. Calhoun's restaurant and so it's good to be here. I hope nobody throws any cornbread out because then the carp will come up. That was one of the biggest things that the world's fair. People would always talk about feeding the carp. They're still here. Yeah, they're still here. Tennessee, the River Dolphins. But here in East Tennessee, you know, I've often said we got some of the best lakes and streams in the country. There's a duck quacking over there. Big impact on our local economy. In addition to fishing, boating, kayaking, of course, and canoeing. It's become more and more popular every year. You know, it's funny when I was a kid, you'd just see me out here with a John Boater, somebody skiing, and now it's just everywhere. Is somebody a kayaker or something, or are those, what do they call those paddle boards? Paddle boards. Stand up paddle boards, yeah. I'm yet to master that, have you? I haven't tried yet. All right. Well, it's probably a good way to embarrass yourself, I guess. Of course, our waterways, they played an important role, moving materials and goods to the region. When I was county mayor, I would appear to city county building, and what in a day go by when the big barges would come down, hauling rebar or something, some other product down the river and it was... It's a good thing that we manage them so well. Of course, I've been committed to supporting these water resources from my work on the Transportation Infrastructure Committee, and we're bringing awareness to the threat of invasive carp and other species that are on our rivers and lakes, and to me that is Poses one of the biggest threats to our ecosystem, I think, than anything, because they populate some fast, and they eat everything. Have y'all heard any reports of them on the Tennessee River yet?",
